enerator\n succeeded in 0ms:\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/README.md\nrunbooks/F014.md\nrunbooks/README.md\nrunbooks/TEMPLATE.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/README.md\n---\ntask_id: T001\nsequence: 1\ntitle: Describe one small, reviewable change\nbrowser_impact: none\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nDescribe the smallest complete outcome. State what is in scope and, equally\nimportant, what must not be changed.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- The intended behaviour is implemented and documented where it affects users or developers.\n- Focused automated tests cover the change and pass.\n- No unrelated refactors, network calls, or credentials are introduced.\n---\ntask_id: F014\nsequence: 14\ntitle: Generate the bounded implementation-runbook authoring collection\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nGenerate only the bootstrap wave of ordinary Supervisor runbooks in\n`projects/<project-slug>/authoring-runbooks/`: a bounded catalogue-expansion\ntask followed by a dispatcher task. The dispatcher allocates at most ten\nauthoring B-series tasks per wave, creates one successor dispatcher, and stops.\nEach authoring task instructs Codex to create only its assigned R-series\nimplementation runbooks under\n`projects/<project-slug>/runbooks/`, then validate their metadata and update the\nmanifest. The successor dispatcher repeats this process until no queued\ncatalogue chapter or unassigned implementation contract remains. Generate an\n`INITIAL.md` context document in `authoring-runbooks/`\nthat points to the canonical `PROJECT_BRIEF.md` and specification.\n\nRegister the generated authoring collection with the source collection by\nwriting `runbooks/.supervisor-children/<project-slug>.json` containing\n`{\"runbooks_dir\": \"../projects/<project-slug>/authoring-runbooks\"}`. Also\nwrite `authoring-runbooks/.supervisor-children/implementation.json` containing\n`{\"runbooks_dir\": \"../runbooks\"}`. These are explicit child-collection\nregistrations; `supervisor-run --run-all` follows them automatically after the\nparent collection is accepted.\n\nEvery dispatcher first expands at most one queued specification/catalogue domain\nchapter (maximum 250 `IMP-*` contracts), validates its coverage, then allocates\nready contracts. If any queued chapter or ready contract remains, it creates no\nmore than ten authoring tasks and exactly one successor dispatcher. If none\nremains, it creates the final audit task instead. This is a durable loop, not a\nrecursive prompt or an unbounded single run.\n\nEvery generated R-series runbook must use the standard Supervisor front matter\nand contain: precise objective; dependencies; relevant requirement IDs; owned\nfiles/data/interfaces; required behaviour; non-goals; platform differences;\nverification commands/evidence; and observable acceptance criteria. It must be\nsmall enough for one implementation task, not a vague epic.\n\nEvery generated R-series runbook must retain machine-readable provenance to the\ncanonical decisions that justify it. Its front matter must declare all of these\nnon-empty fields, using comma-separated values where more than one applies:\n\n```yaml\nsource_specifications: specification/03-technical-contract.md#save-state\nsource_catalogue_ids: IMP-SAVE-001\nauthoring_batch: B0007\nfactory_stages: F003,F005,F012,F013\n```\n\n`source_specifications` points to the smallest relevant canonical\nspecification section; `source_catalogue_ids` identifies the allocated\nimplementation-catalogue records; `authoring_batch` identifies the B-series\nwriter that created the R file; and `factory_stages` identifies the F-series\nstages that established the requirement. This is a traceability trail, not an\ninstruction to inject every F or B runbook into the implementation agent's\nprompt. The R task uses these links to read only the canonical context it needs.\n\nEvery generated R-series runbook must also include a detailed asset assessment.\nIts front matter must always declare both fields below, even when no assets are\nneeded:\n\n```yaml\nasset_impact: required # or not_applicable\nasset_ids: village_gate_main,village_gate_construction # blank only when not_applicable\n```\n\nFor `asset_impact: required`, the B-series writer must reserve stable,\nproduct-specific asset IDs (never generic names such as `image1`), provide an\n`asset_brief` and `visual_style_version`, and include an `## Asset assessment`\nsection explaining the assets' purpose, where they appear, the required visual\nstates/variants, source-of-truth ownership, and original-art acceptance checks.\nFor `not_applicable`, that section must explain why the scope needs no generated\nor source visual asset and explicitly prohibit incidental asset creation. This\nkeeps UI, illustration, icon, texture, sprite, audio-visual, and other product\nasset work visible in the implementation plan instead of discovering it midway\nthrough coding.\n\nEvery generated R-series runbook must also declare an audio assessment, even\nwhen audio is not needed:\n\n```yaml\naudio_impact: required # or not_applicable\naudio_ids: ember_menu_loop # blank only when not_applicable\naudio_brief: 30-second seamless instrumental menu cue # blank only when not_applicable\naudio_duration_seconds: 30 # 0 only when not_applicable\naudio_loop: required # required, not_required, or not_applicable\naudio_style_version: project-audio-v1\n```\n\nFor `audio_impact: required`, the writer must reserve stable cue IDs, cite the\nrelevant `04-audio-direction.md` section, describe the in-product trigger and\nmix/accessibility expectations, and assign a sensible duration and loop policy.\nThe Supervisor's separate local audio lane then creates a manifest, invokes\nACE-Step, and performs technical/provenance QA before coding begins. For\n`not_applicable`, every audio field has the stated empty/default form so an\notherwise unrelated task cannot silently generate music or sound effects.\n\nEach B-series authoring task may create at most seven R-series implementation\nrunbooks. It must stop after its assigned seven (or fewer), update the manifest,\nand leave remaining work for the next bounded batch.\n\nAn unresolved framework, legal, service, design, or other human gate is never\npermission for a B-series writer to omit its assigned R files or wait for an\nearlier B batch to be accepted. The writer produces the bounded contract now,\nrecords the gate and any R-to-R implementation dependency explicitly, and lets\nthe later R-series product-work run pause at that genuine decision. In short:\n**gates block implementation, not runbook authoring**.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- The initial collection contains only a bounded bootstrap wave; each\n  dispatcher expands at most one 250-contract domain shard, creates at most ten\n  new B-series authoring tasks plus one successor, then exits. It never attempts\n  to materialise the whole game plan.\n- There is exactly one valid B-series authoring runbook per allocated manifest\n  batch, and it creates no more than seven assigned R-series runbooks.\n- B-series runbooks never overwrite an existing R-series contract with a\n  conflicting ID; they stop and report mismatches.\n- Every generated B-series runbook includes its context packet, output list,\n  validation procedure, checkpoint/update procedure, and a clear stop condition.\n- Every generated B-series runbook states that it must author its allocated R\n  files despite unresolved implementation gates or unimplemented R\n  dependencies, retaining those gates in the R contract rather than stopping.\n- Both child-collection registration files exist, contain valid JSON with a\n  relative `runbooks_dir`, and point to the generated authoring and\n  implementation collections respectively.\n- R-series contracts use stable IDs and parse with the Supervisor runbook\n  loader; their declared dependencies match the manifest, every contract has\n  explicit `asset_impact` and `asset_ids` metadata, and asset-required\n  contracts have non-empty stable IDs. Every R contract also has non-empty,\n  valid `source_specifications`, `source_catalogue_ids`, `authoring_batch`,\n  and `factory_stages` provenance metadata.\n- Every R-series contract has explicit valid audio metadata. Audio-required\n  contracts have stable IDs, a non-empty cue brief, duration, loop policy,\n  audio-style version, and a source link to the canonical audio direction.\n- The collection contains a final audit B-series task after all authoring\n  batches and catalogue expansions are complete, rather than pretending that\n  thousands of contracts can be reviewed in one generation step.\nrunbook allocation and checkpoint validation passed\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -C 2 'runbook.*load|front.?matter|yaml|source_specifications|asset_impact' supervisor scripts -g '*.py' -g '*.sh' | head -n 220\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\nscripts/run_factory_e2e.sh-107-import sys\nscripts/run_factory_e2e.sh-108-from pathlib import Path\nscripts/run_factory_e2e.sh:109:from supervisor.runbooks import load_task\nscripts/run_factory_e2e.sh-110-\nscripts/run_factory_e2e.sh-111-root = Path(sys.argv[1])\n--\nscripts/run_factory_e2e.sh-116-for path in runbooks:\nscripts/run_factory_e2e.sh-117-    task = load_task(path)\nscripts/run_factory_e2e.sh:118:    assert task.asset_impact in {'required', 'not_applicable'}\nscripts/run_factory_e2e.sh:119:    if task.asset_impact == 'required':\nscripts/run_factory_e2e.sh-120-        assert task.asset_ids, f'{path} needs stable asset_ids'\nscripts/run_factory_e2e.sh-121-print(f'E2E PASS: {len(runbooks)} valid R-series runbooks in {root}')\n--\nsupervisor/supervisor/manage.py-21-from dotenv import dotenv_values\nsupervisor/supervisor/manage.py-22-\nsupervisor/supervisor/manage.py:23:from .runbooks import load_task\nsupervisor/supervisor/manage.py-24-\nsupervisor/supervisor/manage.py-25-\n--\nsupervisor/sup